[StudyName "Reti - Reversed Benko"]

1. Nf3 d5 2. c4 d4 3. g3 c5 4. b4 cxb4 5. a3 Nc6 (5... e6! { This is more dangerous than other variations where black plays e6. Now they have the option to defend the b-pawn with a5. } { [%cal Ga7a5] } 6. Bg2 Nc6 7. O-O Nf6 8. Bb2 a5! { [Critical]
This seems to be one of the most critical variations, looking at the win-rates and eval. } (8... bxa3 { Black decides to take now that we've spent a tempi on Bg7 - Bxa3 is less tempting. } 9. Nxa3 { e5 here will likely transpose: (9.... e5 10. e3 Bc5) } 9... Bc5 (9... Be7 10. Nb5 { Adding another attacker on d4. } { [%cal Gb5d4,Gf3d4,Gb2d4] } 10... e5 11. e3 Bg4 12. exd4 e4 { Black can also take the pawn, but it doesn't look as critical: (12... exd4 13. h3 Bxf3 14. Bxf3 O-O 15. Bxc6 bxc6 16. Nxd4) } 13. d5 exf3 (13... Nb4 14. Bxf6 Bxf6 { If black tries the other way: (14... exf3 15. Bxe7 fxg2 16. Bxd8 Bxd1 17. Rfxd1) } 15. Qb1 exf3 16. Qxb4 { [%cal Gf1e1] } 16... fxg2 17. Rfe1+) 14. Bxf3 Bxf3 15. Qxf3 Nb4 16. d6 Bxd6 17. Nxd6+ Qxd6 18. Rfe1+ Kf8 19. Qxb7 { [%cal Gb2a3,Ba8b8,Bb7a7] }) 10. e3 O-O 11. exd4 Nxd4 12. Nxd4 Bxd4 13. Bxd4 Qxd4 14. Qe2 { Defending the c-pawn so that the knight can move. Also connecting the rooks. } { [%csl Gc4,Ge2][%cal Ba3b5,Bf1b1,Bf1d1] } 14... a6 { Another likely continuation is Rd8 where we can play Nb5 without actually hanging the d-pawn: (Rd8 15. Nb5 Qxd2 16. Rfd1) } 15. Rfb1 Rb8 16. Nc2 Qd6 17. d4 { The position is equal. Although there are many ways to go wrong: (17... Rd8 18. Nb4 Qxd4 19. Rd1 Qb6 20. Qe3) } { [%cal Gb7b5,Gc4c5,Bf8d8,Bc2b4] }) (8... e5 { Looks strange to move the pawn twice, but it's one of the best moves for black. } 9. e3 bxa3 10. Nxa3 Bc5 11. Nxe5 Nxe5 12. exd4 Bxd4 13. Bxd4 Qxd4 14. Nb5 Qd8 { The other queen moves are no better: (14... Qd7 15. Qe2 O-O 16. Qxe5 Qxd2 17. Nc7)(14... Qb6 15. Qe2)(14... Qc5 15. d4) } 15. Qe2 Qe7 16. d4 Nc6 17. Nc7+ Kd8 18. Qxe7+ Nxe7 19. Nxa8 { The knight will escape as our rook is threatening Rxa7. } { [%csl Ga7][%cal Ga1a7,Ba7a6,Ba8b6] }) 9. e3 dxe3 10. dxe3 Qxd1 (10... Bd7 11. Ne5 Nxe5 12. Bxe5 Bc6 13. Bxc6+ bxc6 14. Qf3) 11. Rxd1 Be7 12. axb4 { Now that the bishop has moved - we capture. } 12... Bxb4 { If knight takes: (12... Nxb4 13. Ne5 O-O 14. Nc3 h6 15. Ba3) } 13. Nc3 Ke7 { We continue the same way if black castles } 14. Na4 { With plans of Nb6. If black stops is it with Ra6, we can respond with c5. (15... Ra6 c5).

Remember, Rd8 is pretty much always bad here when the rooks aren't connected: (14... Rd8 15. Rxd8 Kxd8 16. Rd1+) }) (5... b3 6. Bg2 Nc6 7. O-O e5 8. d3 Nf6 9. Qxb3 Be7 10. a4 O-O 11. Ba3 Nd7 12. Bxe7 Qxe7 13. a5 Nc5 14. Qa3) 6. axb4 Nxb4 7. Qa4+ Nc6 8. Ne5 Bd7 (8... Qc7 { Black attacks our knight. However, it's simply a waste of tempo as we can ignore it... } 9. Bg2 Bd7 { Taking the knight is bad for black: (9... Qxe5 10. Bxc6+ Kd8 11. Bf3) } 10. Nxd7 Qxd7 { Basically, black wasted a tempo on Qc7 and we've transposed into a better version of the Bd7 line } 11. O-O e5 12. d3 Nf6 13. Bg5 Be7 { [%cal Gg5f6] } 14. Nd2 O-O 15. Rfb1 { We start with Rb1 instead of Nb3 when blacks bishop is still e7 covering c5. Instead of going for a quick Nc5, we put pressure on b7. } 15... Rfc8 16. Nb3 { Next we'll go for Na5 unless black provokes the bishop trade or stops us with b6, which is the best attempt for black: () } { [%cal Gb3a5,Rb7b6,Bh7h6,Bg5f6,Bb3c5] } 16... b6 { Example if black doesn't play b6: (16... h6 17. Bxf6 gxf6 18. Na5 Rab8 19. Rxb7 Rxb7 20. Bxc6 Rxc6 21. Qxc6) } 17. Nd2 { Blacks stops our plan of Nc5 and Na5 - but b6 is not without problems.

If black goes for our bishop: (17... h6 18. Bxf6 Bxf6 19. c5 bxc5 20. Rb5 Be7 21. Nc4 f6 22. Ra5)

Other possible tactical opportunities: (17... Rc7 18. Rxb6 axb6 19. Qxa8+)(17... Rab8 18. Bxc6 Rxc6 19. Qxa7 Rb7 20. Qa6) } { [%cal Gh7h6,Gg5f6,Gc4c5] }) 9. Nxd7 Qxd7 10. Bg2 e5 11. O-O Nf6 (11... Bd6 12. d3 Nge7 13. Nd2 O-O 14. Ne4 { We don't have to take the bishop if they let us. 
There are a lot of tactical traps in the position.
For example: (14... Bc7 15. Ba3 Rfe8 16. Nc5 Qc8 17. Nxb7 Qxb7 18. Bxe7 Rxe7 19. Bxc6)(14... Rfd8 15. Rb1 Rab8 16. f4 exf4 17. Nxd6 Qxd6 18. Bxf4 Ne5 19. Qxa7) } { [%cal Gd6b4,Gc1a3,Bf7f5,Be4d6,Bf1b1,Ba1b1] }) (11... Bc5 12. d3 { We go for the same plan as the Bd6 variation - getting the knight to e4. } { [%cal Gd2d3,Bb1d2,Bd2e4] } 12... Nge7 13. Nd2 f5 { Black stops our plan, but it's a mistake } { [%csl Re4][%cal Rf5e4] } 14. Qb5 { b6 walks into Nb3 -> c5.
Bd6 walks into c5.
The best attempt is: (14... Bb4 15. Nf3 Qd6 16. Nxe5 Qxe5 17. Bxc6+ Nxc6 18. Qxb7 O-O 19. Ra6) and both pieces can't be protected. } { [%cal Gb7b6,Gd2b3,Bc4c5,Bc5d6] }) 12. d3 Be7 13. Bg5 O-O 14. Nd2 h6 { Same plan applies if black goes after the bishop with the queen: (14... Qf5 15. Bxf6 Qxf6 16. Rfb1) } 15. Bxf6 Bxf6 16. Nb3 { Going for Nc5. If black stops it with Be7 we have Na5: (16... Be7 17. Na5 Rfc8 18. Rfb1 Rab8 19. Rxb7 Rxb7 20. Bxc6) with some tactical possibilities.

Here we start with Nb3 before Rb1 because blacks bishop is on f6 instead of e7. So Nc5 is a bigger threat. } { [%cal Gb3c5,Gf1b1,Bf6e7,Bb3a5] } *
